<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#darughachi">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Darughachi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as daruqachi, basqaq, baskak, dārugheh, daruga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A darughachi (in Turkic basqaq or baskak) was an official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> responsible for taxes and administration in a province, a division called a darugha. The title was established under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> from 1211, and after the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> and the Russian principalities in 1237–1240 Ögödei placed darughachi over peoples whose cities included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolghar">Bolghar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, where they were called baskaki, they supervised the collection of tribute, but were withdrawn by 1328, when the grand prince of Vladimir gathered it for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. In Goryeo the darughachi stationed after the invasion of 1231 were all killed in the summer of 1232.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Rus' it conducted censuses in the 1250s, placed <b>darughachi</b> and basqaq officials to supervise tribute and recruitment, and granted princes patents (yarlyk) to rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Conquered peoples supplied bureaucrats such as the Khitan minister Yelü Chucai, engineers and tax collectors, while resident overseers known as <b>darughachi</b> supervised local rulers and revenue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In each district a <b>darughachi</b>, a Mongol or semu overseer, served beside the Chinese administrators.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li></ul></section>
